#{[17592186444792 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186444896 "![](images/55/555eec8e88dd658903e19b8779f85598f57126b054d5058b662824f09de0f6cb.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,0]$"] [17592186444897 "![](images/12/12acb33aa174f1c174a538a89881dd19245f733c6b39df37f9ebbdc1f86442d5.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-3,1]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({2}^{x}-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186445483 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1, \\\\ {e}^{(x-y)},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(0,2)\\times (0,1)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186445486 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<1, \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<1,\\,1<y<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(-1,1)\\times (0,2)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186445487 "![](images/bc/bcc2eb6cd27450375016677390700675e9733c03dfd2c0100622e6f8c7d18e78.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186459244 "$T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [17592186459267 "$T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [17592186459301 "$T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?"] [17592186476875 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$"] [17592186476879 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c\\sqrt{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x-{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186476882 "Find $\\text{E}[XY],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186476884 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{(x+y)^{3}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$"] [17592186476885 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{2}+2y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y>2).$"] [17592186476889 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & x\\le 0,\\,y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & y\\le 0 \\\\ 1 & y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$"] [17592186476893 "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y+1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186476905 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P((X-1)^{2}+{Y}^{2}<1).$"] [17592186476909 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle P(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<Y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}).\n$$"] [17592186476911 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$"] [17592186476913 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$"] [17592186476914 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{9}, & 0\\le x\\le 9,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ g(y), & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$"] [17592186476927 "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}8xy,\\, & 0\\le x,\\,0\\le y,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186476931 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$"] [17592186476940 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{X}(x).$"] [17592186476942 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(5x+\\frac{y}{5})}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 5).$"] [17592186476948 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$"] [17592186476949 "Find $\\text{E}[Y\\sqrt{X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2{y}^{3}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186476954 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{21}(xy)^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$"] [17592186476959 "Let $D=[1,e]\\times [1,e].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-y}{4e},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{xy},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186476960 "Find $\\text{E}[Y\\operatorname{cos}⁡X],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186476967 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{{e}^{X-Y}}{1-X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186476969 "Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X-Y)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le x; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186476973 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{Y}{7X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}y,\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186476977 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c,\\, & 0\\le y<1-x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186478374 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{7}(x+\\sqrt{y}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{7},$ find $\\text{E}[2X+Y].$"] [17592186478381 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1.5,Y\\ge 1).$ Round your answer to three decimal places."] [17592186478387 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}({y}^{2}+1), & \\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*"] [17592186478394 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+1), & \\,2\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 4).$"] [17592186478396 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}y, & \\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,1\\le Y\\le 3).$"] [17592186478405 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(x+2y), & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{17}{30},$ find $\\text{E}[X+2Y].$"] [17592186478414 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186478421 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [17592186478423 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}, & \\,0\\le y, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3).$"] [17592186478424 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+2), & \\,1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$"] [17592186478427 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{5},$ find $\\text{E}[5X-Y].$"] [17592186478431 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(y+1), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186478439 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}$ for $y\\in [0,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [17592186478444 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(y-1), & \\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*"] [17592186478451 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find $\\text{E}[X+Y].$"] [17592186478458 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y{e}^{-3x},\\, & 0\\le x,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [17592186478463 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{21}, & -3\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ find $\\text{E}[3X+7Y].$"] [17592186479070 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$"] [17592186480675 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [17592186480684 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ x+1, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)